-In \OSCMASP{}, the admission decision is not based only on whether $ready(b)$ has high posterior probability. It also depends on whether the support set for $ready(b)$ is undefeated under the dominance scheme, whether contradictory relevant claims remain active, and whether the action is critical enough to require certification or escalation. This running example is used throughout the paper: the minimal benchmark in \cref{sec:benchmark} is the smallest stochastic game that preserves this structure while stripping away full-port complexity.
+The conflict is not only that $ready(b)$ and $\neg ready(b)$ coexist. Existing methods already have mechanisms for uncertainty, disagreement, and partial observation. The distinctive issue is that the contradiction is \emph{typed by provenance}: the formulas are asserted by different observers, at different situation nodes, with different authority and freshness. A controller that collapses the table to a posterior probability may preserve enough information to choose a high expected-reward action, but it may lose the information required to decide whether that action is admissible, certifiable, or must be routed through an exception.
+
+\begin{example}[Two superficially identical aggregates]
+\label{ex:two-aggregates}
+Consider two claim bases with the same formulas and timestamps:
+\[
+C^+=\{(ready,\ell_H,1,0,\rho_1),(\neg ready,\ell_L,1,0,\rho_2)\},
+\]
+\[
+C^-=\{(ready,\ell_L,1,0,\rho_1),(\neg ready,\ell_H,1,0,\rho_2)\},
+\]
+where $\ell_H$ is dominant over $\ell_L$ for readiness claims. A provenance-erased controller sees the same aggregate in both cases. An observer--situation-aware controller sees that $C^+$ supports \texttt{admit} and $C^-$ supports \texttt{hold}. The separation theorem in \cref{sec:theory} is the stochastic-game version of this example.
+\end{example}
+
+This example also clarifies what the paper is \emph{not} claiming. It is not claiming that all multi-source conflicts require symbolic provenance, nor that every maritime decision should be passed through a theorem prover. The claim is narrower: in regulated, safety- or service-critical multi-agent control, there are regimes in which provenance-bearing contradictions are themselves part of the state of the control problem. In those regimes, observer--situation labels are not post-hoc explanation metadata; they affect admissibility, regret, and constraint tradeoffs.
